A||||34||Cliff Pennock||New speakers, worse sound...||||9||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||08-22-2001||02:24 PM||||Amongst the things I bought when I was in the States a few weeks ago, were new front and rear speakers for my Fiero. The "original" speakers were in there since I bought the car and although I had never taken a look at them, they sounded like they were at the end of their lives.<P>Last week I finally replaced them. The rear speakers were the <B>Alpine 6204</B> (40 watt max ) and they were indeed pretty damaged. The front speakers were <B>Alpine 6210</B> (60 watt max) and they still looked pretty good although at high volumes they distorted a little bit. I replace the front speakers with the <B>Pioneer TS-A4103</B> (120 watt max) and the rear speakers with the <B>Pioneer TS-G4611</B> (65 watt). After I had hooked them up, they actually sounded worse then the old Alpines! Although the front speakers now have twice the wattage of the old speakers, the Pioneers start distorting at <B>much</B> lower volumes. And it has much less bass then the Alpines. The rear speakers sound the same as the old ones, even though the old ones were pretty damaged!<P>Everything is connected properly (so none of the speakers is out of phase) but I can not believe how much worse these speakers sound. Before I had bought the Pioneers, I had read some reviews about them and they were all - without exception - very positive.<P>Now are the Pioneers junk or are the Alpines so good? Anyone has any experience with either brand?<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||87GTBro||08-22-2001||02:26 PM||||It could be that those pioneers were designed to be installed in an enclosed box and don't work so well as an open-air type speaker.  You should check into it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||p4n1c||08-22-2001||02:36 PM||||I have Pioneer's in the front and back of my Cutlass.  The rears are open to the trunk, the bass coming out of them rivals some subs that people have had in their cars at my house.  I'm pretty happy with them, they sound really good. <P>I forget the model numbers, but the rears are 2-way 200 watt 6x9s and the fronts are 40 watt 4".  I want to replace the fronts with two ways and put the exisiting ones in the door to replace the factories there.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Joe Torma||08-22-2001||02:49 PM||||What kind of head unit do you have Cliff?  The Pioneers like power.  I ran 65watts RMS though my Pioneer 4x10s and they sounded awesome.<P>Your old speakers may be a better match for your head unit's output.  It may be the head unit trying too hard.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||08-22-2001||02:53 PM||||Cliff,<P>This won't be a popular opinion... but the new speakers sound inferior, because they are.  Pioneer speakers are horrible at reproducing accurate sound.  Did you buy them at a national chain so someone could return them for you?  (e-mail your response to me... I rarely come here anymore)<P>William||||reg||1
Z||000005||p4n1c||08-22-2001||03:16 PM||||I have an Alpine head unit 40x4 and my Pioneer's sound fine.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||08-22-2001||03:30 PM||||Stock radio?<BR>Might not have enough power to drive them!<BR>Radio may be trash too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||08-22-2001||03:38 PM||||The head unit is the <A HREF="http://www.pioneerelectronics.com/Pioneer/CDA/CarProducts/CarProductDetails/0,1429,521,00.html" TARGET=_blank>Pioneer DEH-P77DH</A>. I also checked the Pioneer speaker's specs and they <B>are</B> open air speakers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cliff Pennock||08-22-2001||03:41 PM||||BTW, here are the links for the speakers: <A HREF="http://www.pioneerelectronics.com/Pioneer/CDA/CarProducts/CarProductDetails/0,1429,60,00.html" TARGET=_blank>TS-G4611</A> and <A HREF="http://www.pioneerelectronics.com/Pioneer/CDA/CarProducts/CarProductDetails/0,1429,28,00.html" TARGET=_blank>TS-A4103</A>.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fieronv||08-22-2001||03:46 PM||||Cliff:<BR> You can do as much damage to a speaker by under-powering them as over-powering. If the head unit only is rated for 15 watts and you have to bring the volume up above 3/4 to get the sound level you want you can begin to clip the audio (distortion) and even though the speakers are rated at 80 watts you can damage them. It is better to check the specifications of the head and see if you need an external amplifier to power the speakers. Personally I am not a fan of Pioneer but have heard some nice systems that use Pioneer. I know that alpine are more efficient than pioneer (1 watt= 91db for pioneer and 94db for alpine) what this means that the Alpines do not need as much power to get the same listen level as the pioneers. I would personally do a couple of things <BR>1. Get a small 4 channel amp that is rated the same as the speakers.<BR>2. Invest in some damping foam to place behind the speakers.<BR>Both of these things are a minimal expense and can be ordered over the net. Good Luck and keep us posted. <P>------------------<BR>85GT (twoloud)||||reg||6
Z||000010||JayUSA77||08-22-2001||03:50 PM||||hook a small amp to the speakers and they sound great, plus i have my car spread with dynamat so there is no distortion.<BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||08-22-2001||03:51 PM||||Cliff,<P>Maybe the Alpines are just <I>that good</I>.<P>I have a pair of the TS-A4103's in my car and they were a drastic improvement over the stock Fiero speakers. Although, to be fair, the originals were prety much toasted. They distort at high volume but they're not terrible, by any means.<BR>This is behind a Delco UP3 (old style) CD.<P>I have a DEHP77 that's going in my Sonoma, in the not-too-distant future. I also have another pair of TS-A4103's that are going in, too. I'll let you know how it works out.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
