Z||000031||Raydar||08-23-2001||09:08 AM||||Cliff,<P>Have you tried fading to only the front or only the rear speakers. <BR>Maybe they built a batch that had the coils wound backwards or something, and the rears are cancelling the front. <BR>You probably have checked this already, but it's just my "shot in the dark".<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Cliff Pennock||08-23-2001||09:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Have you tried fading to only the front or only the rear speakers. <BR>Maybe they built a batch that had the coils wound backwards or something, and the rears are cancelling the front. <BR>You probably have checked this already, but it's just my "shot in the dark".<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, that's always the first thing I do after I install speakers - to make sure nothing is contra-phased.||||reg||1
Z||000033||Alex4mula||08-23-2001||09:40 AM||||I have the same DEH-P77Dh unit as you with Pioneer at front and I guess stock in the back (never seen them but sound terrible). Bass reproduction caused a lot of distortion mainly in the back but also in the front. <BR>What I did was to set the DEH internal crossover to 80Hz. I use the loudness setting at MID level. Now I can pump up the volume with little distortion and get acceptable bass. Still no substitute to a good sub. ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Roy Grooms||08-25-2001||10:30 AM||||Cliff.<BR>    I sold fiero,s when they came out,back in 84 and the stock speakers are set up for the space in the car,along with the amps<BR> I am not a radio nut! but I heard a lot of the changed ones and the stock one in good shape seem to me to be the best<BR>      Roystoy<BR>      84 auto 2m4 (I up grade the radio a little w/ more amps and they still sounded great)<BR>         ||||reg||1
